  "id": "DrugBank_DB01214 - MONDO_6875",
  "verbalization": "Metipranolol, a beta-blocker, is implicated in the pathogenesis of ocular hypertension through its association with open-angle glaucoma, a condition that is itself linked to ocular hypertension. The evidence suggests that Metipranolol may exert a role in the development of ocular hypertension via its effects on the eye, potentially through mechanisms involving the regulation of intraocular pressure. The observed indications for Metipranolol in open-angle glaucoma, which is a known contributor to ocular hypertension, provide a direct pathway for its potential involvement in the condition. The shared indication of open-angle glaucoma across multiple paths highlights a possible common mechanism or pathway that connects Metipranolol to ocular hypertension. The presence of similar indications for other beta-blockers, such as Befunolol and Levobetaxolol, further supports the idea that beta-blockers may influence ocular hypertension through a shared biological mechanism, possibly involving the modulation of intraocular pressure or other systemic effects. The integrated evidence suggests that Metipranolol may contribute to the development of ocular hypertension through its effects on the eye, though the exact mechanism remains to be fully elucidated.",
